WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:04.350
Film Courage: Adam do you outline every story
that you write?

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.790
Adam Skelter, writer, director, story artist:
Yeah…I’m pretty thorough with outlining.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:18.560
I’ll spend quite a bit of time outlining
before I even write the full…like I’ll

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.220
usually have a complete full outline and then
jump into the story.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.849
So I’ll have a really specific idea of the
objective of every single scene and ultimately

00:00:27.849 --> 00:00:29.609
it saves me time.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:31.810
When I first started writing I was anti-outline.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:38.430
I kind of had the attitude, you know the young
attitude I’m just going to write and my

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.080
gut’s going to tell me what’s right and
what’s meaningful and stuff like that and

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.970
that works for a lot of writers.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.890
But I noticed I hit a lot of dead ends and
I became emotionally attached to those dead

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:55.300
ends and then it became very hard to rewrite
and then it drove my stories off the cliff.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.370
And so little by little, honestly that Ron
Mita 24-plot-point-thing was huge because

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.720
it was so simple.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:07.810
You could literally just sit there and plot
out a story in an hour and you could have

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.600
a really good idea about where it was going.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.970
And then from there…for me that’s where
you go from like discovery your story to actually

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.180
becoming a craftsman (or craftsperson) where
you are working on the story and start to

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.040
take control of it and you take a step back.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:29.670
It’s almost like a render machine, you’re
rendering your story before you ever sit down

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:30.670
and write it.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.730
So then the script really just becomes kind
of like secondary artifact of all the work

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.570
you’ve put into it.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.460
So yeah, I spend a lot of time outlining.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:47.060
And it’s…a lot of people thinks that it
takes away from the creative process.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.240
Usually that’s kind of the writer saying
“Well, I want to be in the audience, too.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.740
And I want to experience and be surprised
by it.”

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.030
But outlining doesn’t take away from that
because when you sit down you’ve outlined,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.430
you’ve got your outline right here.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.260
And then when you’re writing, you put yourself
in the moment, you’re in the skin of the

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.140
writer or the character.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.630
And you are experiencing it and then if your
gut tells you try something different, try

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.720
something different, see where it goes.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.410
So you are still…you know…gardener versus
architect, passer versus plotter kind of thing.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:27.810
I call it The Assassins versus The Berserkers.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:33.940
Basically the planner versus the person who
just sees what happens next.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:41.280
And I think it’s important to understand
the consequences of the choices you’re making.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.240
But the whole process of writing is an experiment,
art is an experiment.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:51.790
The only thing that rules it is do we make
it emotionally resonate for somebody else.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.970
And as artists we get to decide how much that
is.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:56.020
How we build that relationship.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:57.020
If.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:01.930
We want to do it for a living than we become
responsible to those people who are investing

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:07.440
in us and that’s when you want to be able
to have control of your craft.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.490
That’s when you want to be able to climb
the mountain, know how you’re going to get

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.730
up there, meet a deadline.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.160
There aren’t a lot of producers that are
going to be like “Alright, we needed this

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.160
three months ago.”

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.250
And you are still just kind of like wandering
in the weeds.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.630
But a lot of the process of writing is just
experimenting into the weeds, going into the

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.379
weeds and then stepping back.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:33.069
And the trick is just not panicking when you’re
in the weeds and saying “Okay, this is part

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:34.069
of the process.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:38.460
Let’s step back and look at the outline
and see if it’s going to work.”

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.709
So for me it feels like a map, it feels like
you’re zooming in.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.300
And you can do the street view where you are
in the character and you’re driving along

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.910
and you’re like “Yeah, I feel this, I’m
going through this street.”

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.190
And that’s when you’re writing.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.489
But then you pull back, you zoom out and you
see the overview of where you’re going and

00:03:56.489 --> 00:03:58.380
that’s the outline.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:04.450
Film Courage: So do you think without an outline
a writer back themselves into a corner a little

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:05.450
bit?

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.900
Adam Skelter: I mean we’re all vulnerable
to that, even with an outline we’re vulnerable

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.019
to that.

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:15.379
But again, I don’t believe in any right
or wrong

00:04:15.379 --> 00:04:17.329
There is this one writer Bob Sáenz.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:22.100
He’s amazing, he says he doesn’t outline,
he’s successful, he writes great stuff.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.689
He just wrote Extracurricular and it’s fantastic.

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:29.159
I read the script and it’s so good.

00:04:29.159 --> 00:04:33.849
And he doesn’t outline but he knows how
to write.

00:04:33.849 --> 00:04:39.309
He gets to where he needs to go and he knows
how to tell a story.

00:04:39.309 --> 00:04:42.620
So it’s like what works for you is fine.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:49.409
The important thing is to be able to sell
it to the producers, to engage the people

00:04:49.409 --> 00:04:50.409
that need to be engaged.

00:04:50.409 --> 00:04:52.240
You are writing for readers largely.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.050
So you want to learn the language of what
they expect.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.830
But within that, it’s whatever gets you
there.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:03.930
I don’t think you have any obligation but
generally speaking the average person would

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:09.860
benefit from taking the time to plan where
their story is going to go, have a good idea

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:13.520
of where it’s going to end and work toward
that.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.360
Film Courage: In the past have you introduced
that 24-plot-point system to some of your

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:20.990
students and were they surprised?

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.830
Adam Skelter: Yeah, in my first video [on
Adam’s Youtube channel THE ART OF STORY]

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.259
I introduce the 24-plot-points and then I
break it down into acts, and then sequences

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:31.110
and then scenes and then everything like that.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:37.509
So it’s like a really simple…a lot of
people they will stare at the blank page.

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:39.559
I’ve never understood staring at the blank
page.

00:05:39.559 --> 00:05:44.129
Because by the time I’m ready to write a
scene, I’ve already had pages of writing.

00:05:44.129 --> 00:05:51.139
It’s like the slingshot, you are just like
slowly preparing, getting all of the scenes,

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:55.509
getting the character, getting the plot, the
conflict, you know what every character wants.

00:05:55.509 --> 00:06:00.399
So when you sit down and are ready to write,
you let go and you’re just flying.

00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:07.129
But it really comes down to everybody discovering
their own process and I’m not a purist.

00:06:07.129 --> 00:06:10.669
And a lot of people don’t outline and they
are very good writers, they are very successful.

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:13.789
A lot of that is the intuition or their experience
that informs it.

